    "narrative": "Our offensive outlook today is anchored by **James Wood**, whose 78.42 efficiency score makes him our clear standout. Facing Lucas Giolito and his generous 5.37 xERA, **James Wood** benefits from the prime leadoff spot and a strong xwOBA of 0.384, positioning him as our primary engine for Runs and RBI. Close behind are **Austin Riley** (65.80) and **Josh Naylor** (64.49). **Austin Riley** is enjoying a significant SP skill advantage against Bailey Paddack, while **Josh Naylor** overcomes the tough matchup against Zac Gallen thanks to high-leverage lineup placement and favorable platoon dynamics. **Corbin Carroll** remains a critical component of our strategy; despite facing the formidable George Kirby, his 63.34 score is buoyed by an elite 87% stolen base probability against the pitcher\u2019s delivery, making him our highest-upside asset in a category where we refuse to concede ground.\n\nThe optimizer\u2019s most difficult exclusion today was **Cole Young**, whose 44.66 efficiency score placed him just shy of the starting threshold. While **Cole Young** possesses the talent to contribute, the bottleneck created by the **Jose Caballero** and **Brice Turang** defensive/speed tandem in the middle infield pushed him to the bench, as their higher projected total contributions\u2014specifically regarding stolen base upside\u2014took precedence. We are monitoring the status of several \"Lineup Pending\" designations, particularly **Gabriel Moreno** and **Dalton Rushing**. Both catchers face elite arms in Kirby and Zack Wheeler, respectively; while **Dalton Rushing** receives a slight boost from wind conditions, the sub-41 scores for both reflect the reality of starting catchers against top-tier pitching. We remain cautious with **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **Zach McKinstry**, both of whom are hovering near our 40-point efficiency floor due to platoon disadvantages and are candidates for late-game defensive substitution should the game script turn against us.",
